save(73,t,ch,d,inv,'Manuscript page 4; archival 72. The page introduces a 1-connected space T mapped continuously to X, a common group Pi1(X;T), canonical identifications with the groups at p(x), and the canonical path l_y,x induced from T. The optional injective case is explicit: “le cas injectif sera le plus intéressant” and later “mais naturellement p sera injective.” The oblique margin extends phi_x to profinite completions and explicitly refers to (15). It explains how the following Galois expressions may use the same symbol pi without a written hat; a hat is not supplied. Formula (17) identifies the transported f and g values in that common group. Its overwritten number remains doubtful; 14 and 16 and the internal reference 14 are visually readable over older strokes. The unique-arrow wording literally has x:y. De remains live before a Hom whose deletion state is not securely separable from its baseline. The earlier cancelled setup retains the unresolved word Soient and cancelled Pi1(X).',checks=[('142-073-canonical-paths','targeted proof check','For nonempty path-connected simply connected T, any two paths between fixed endpoints differ by a null-homotopic loop. Their images under p therefore give one class l_y,x, with l_z,y l_y,x=l_z,x. This checks the canonical transport assertion under the standard meaning of 1-connected used here; no lifting or injectivity is needed.'),('142-073-f-g-identification','targeted proof check','The definitions give g_l=l f_l l^-1. The common identifications satisfy phi_y=Ad(l_y,x) phi_x by canonical transport. Thus phi_y^-1(g_l)=phi_x^-1(f_l), as written in (17). This calculation applies to the completed groups through the marginal extension of phi; it does not place hats in the text.'),('142-073-profinite-extension','carried as background without fault found','The margin asserts extension of the group identifications to profinite completions. The functorial completion construction is used as background, not separately proved in this reading.')],doubts=[('cancelled setup','Soient','word mostly buried under horizontal strike','low'),('cancelled formula before (14)','Pi1(X)','terminal punctuation obscured','low'),('Hom after de',r'\deleted{Hom}','Hom live','medium'),('last display number','(17)','overwritten lower digit not securely separable','low')],hand=[('One-sided arrowhead at a short path shaft','The small turned tip in (16) agrees with the short arrow habit in 068 and 071.','C gave a plain dash.'),('Plain pi used after the completion convention','Both prose and final membership have no hat.','H supplied hats in both positions.'),('Overwritten equation digits','Latest angular 4 and looped 6 can be separated; last 17 remains doubtful.','No consecutive numbering has been imposed.')],notation=[('Pi1(X;T)=pi','Capital Pi and plain pi; phi_x identifies basepoints','Profinite extension is stated in the margin; hats not written.'),('l_y,x','Canonical image of the path class x:y in T','Directed from p(x) to p(y); composition uses reversed written order.')],abbreviations=[('isom','isomorphisme'),('iso','isomorphismes'),('syst.','système'),('=','même'),('NB','nota bene'),('i.e.','id est')])
